WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.660
Good morning, Hank. It's Tuesday.

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:04.640
So until a few days ago the Malheur National Wildlife Refuge in [southeastern] Oregon

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:07.840
was mostly known for its high quality springtime bird watching.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:11.380
But then some people with guns decided to take over the refuge's headquarters.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:16.060
Which is both a big deal, like armed civillians are in control of a federal building,

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:20.340
and not that big of a deal. Like it's not like the building in question is the US capital.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:24.500
Regardless, it gives us occasion to consider some complicated history and how ideas about public

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:27.160
and private property have shaped the United States.

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:31.420
So these days Oregon is often seen as one of the most progressive states in the US.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:35.700
I mean, I judge a city primarily by the quality of its soccer support and its public transportation,

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:40.460
by which measures Portland, [not] Oregon's capital, might be America's greatest city.

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:45.400
But as is often true with American history, the deeper you dig, the more troubling it gets.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:50.020
Okay, so around a thousand years ago the northern Paiute tribe's Wadatika band, moved into

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:54.740
what is now the Malheur National Wildlife Refuge. The first Europeans didn't show up until the 1820s

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:57.440
and their arrival, of course, was catastrophic.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:02.160
Like most American Indian groups the Wadatika were devastated by smallpox and other European diseases.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:07.460
As many as 90% of them died from disease within 30 years of the first European contact.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:13.080
So by the 1840s, thousands of white settlers had made their way west to Oregon via the Oregon trail.

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:18.140
This 2200 mile long wagon route, that today is primarily famous as a video game,

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:20.460
in which you almost always die of dysentery.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.520
The settlers encroached on the northern Paiute lands and there were some conflicts

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:28.580
but things didn't really pick up untill 1850, when the US passed the Donation Land Claim Act.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:35.260
That law granted 320 acres of land to any white man who settled and cultivated the land for at least 4 years.

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:37.380
And married couples could get 640 acres of land.

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:41.440
There were a bunch of laws like this. White settlement in Oregon increased dramatically

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:44.060
and by 1859 it became a state.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:49.320
Quick side note: Oregon's first state constitution contained some of the most shameful language in American history.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:52.940
For example, it outlawed black Americans even visiting Oregon.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:58.260
It was technically illegal for African Americans to move to Oregon until 1926.

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:02.280
But right. So by 1859 there were all these new white settlers in Oregon thanks to the federal

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:06.760
government's land grants. And not to state the obvious or anything, but inherent to the idea that

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:12.360
the US federal government could give land to these white settlers, was the idea that the land in question

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:17.880
was the US government's to give. The idea of federally owned territory is established in the consitution

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:22.160
and even today a lot of the United States is owned by the federal government.

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:27.780
Speaking of which, in 1872 the northern Paiute tribe signed the treaty with the United States government

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:34.040
establishing a 1.8 million acre reservation called - get this - the Malheur Reservation.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:39.200
The US government abided by this treaty for all of 0 minutes with settlers immediately encroaching upon

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:45.260
the reservation. And then, after an 1878 war won by the US army the remaining nothern Paiutes were

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:48.200
taken off their land and forcibly moved to Washington state

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:51.760
About 100 northern Paiute families were allowed to return to southwestern Oregon

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:57.380
at the end of the 19th century but today their reservation is less than 1% of its original size

00:02:57.380 --> 00:03:00.260
and their tribe has just 349 living members.

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:05.920
So in summary: for 800-ish years what is now the Malheur National Wildlife Refuge was northern Paiute land.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:10.380
First, because they were the only people living there and then because they negotiated control over the land

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:13.420
in a treaty with US government. The US government then won a war,

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:18.880
deported the remaining native Americans and took control of the land, which it held untill 1908

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:23.140
when president Teddy Roosevelt declared it to be a refuge for birds.

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:26.700
That Teddy Roosevelt. He loved birds. Especially hunting them.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:30.260
So that was the history of the Malheur National Wildlife Refuge until a few days ago,

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:35.520
when it became the de facto headquarters of a militia claiming that the US government has no right to

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:40.720
own federal land. Hank, there are many compelling arguments to be had about government infringing upon

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:43.320
the rights of citizens, but this is not one of them.

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:48.140
So this militia argues that there is something tyrannical about a representative government telling the people

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:52.880
it supposedly represents to do or not do things on certain land.

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:57.280
Like if the government is of the people and by the people, then how can the government prevent me,

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:00.080
a person, from doing whatever I want on federal land?

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:04.400
The answer, of course, is that the land doesn't just belong to you, it belongs to all of us and collectively

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:06.500
we've decided what to do with it.

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:10.560
This is in accordance with the US constitution, which states pretty explicitly that the US congress

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:15.600
shall have the power to - and I quote - "dispose and make all needful rules and regulations respecting

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:18.680
the territory or other property belonging to the United States".

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:23.440
And throughout the US history the Supreme Court has consistenly upheld the idea of federally owned

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:28.260
and managed land. The counterargument I've seen most often is that land should belong to the people

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:33.280
who use it. Not to the government. Which isn't so different from the way the northern Paiutes treated land

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:39.120
for most of Oregon's human history. However, the people using the land in question would still be

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:44.940
the northern Paiutes if the US army hadn't intervened on behalf of white settlers in the 19th century.

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:50.160
In American history, there is no escaping that fundamental fact - our government took this land

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:56.880
from sea to shining sea. Whether through purchase or war, or forced deportations, or dishonest treaties

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:00.660
and then it apportioned some of that land off as private property.

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:05.540
If the federal government has no rights to federal land and it never did, then they never had the right to give away

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:10.380
millions of acres to white settlers in the 19th century. This would render much of American private property

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:13.060
illegitimate. Including, by the way, probably my house.

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:17.740
Hank, it seems to me that if the Malheur National Wildlife Refuge's headquarters building doesn't belong

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:22.320
to the federal government, that built and paid for it, than that building and the Malheur National Wildlife

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:29.520
Refuge, in fact much of southwestern Oregon belongs not to a militia but to the 349 surviving members

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:32.800
of the tribe that has lived on that land for most of its history.

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:34.000
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

